**Proof.**

Set up coordinates with $D$ at the origin, $BC$ along the $x$-axis, and $AD$ along the $y$-axis. Let
$$D=(0,0),\quad A=(0,a),\quad B=(-b,0),\quad C=(c,0),\qquad a,b,c>0.$$
Since $\triangle ABC$ is acute, the orthocenter $H$ lies inside the triangle. The altitude from $B$ is perpendicular to $AC$ (direction $(c,-a)$), hence has direction $(a,c)$; intersecting $x=0$ gives $H=(0,\,bc/a)$. Acuteness gives $0<bc/a<a$, i.e.
$$a^{2}>bc. \tag{$\star$}$$

The reflection $E=2A-H=(0,\,2a-bc/a)=\bigl(0,\,(2a^{2}-bc)/a\bigr)$. Set $e:=(2a^{2}-bc)/a$, so $E=(0,e)$.

**Circumcircle of $BDE$.** Passing through $D=(0,0)$ it has form $x^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy=0$; from $B$: $u=b$; from $E$: $v=-e$. Thus
$$\omega_{1}:\ x^{2}+y^{2}+bx-ey=0.$$
Since $AC\parallel DX$ and $AC$ has direction $(c,-a)$, write $X=t(c,-a)$. Substituting:
$$t^{2}(a^{2}+c^{2})+t(bc+ea)=0,\qquad ea=2a^{2}-bc,\quad bc+ea=2a^{2},$$
so the nonzero solution is $t=-2a^{2}/(a^{2}+c^{2})$, giving
$$X=\left(\frac{-2a^{2}c}{a^{2}+c^{2}},\ \frac{2a^{3}}{a^{2}+c^{2}}\right).$$

**Circumcircle of $CDE$.** Similarly $\omega_{2}:\ x^{2}+y^{2}-cx-ey=0$. With $DY\parallel AB$ (direction $(-b,-a)$), write $Y=s(-b,-a)$; substituting gives $s=-2a^{2}/(a^{2}+b^{2})$, hence
$$Y=\left(\frac{2a^{2}b}{a^{2}+b^{2}},\ \frac{2a^{3}}{a^{2}+b^{2}}\right).$$

**Circumcircle $\Gamma$ of $ABC$.** Solving $x^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0$ through $A,B,C$ yields $u=b-c$, $w=-bc$, $v=(bc-a^{2})/a$. Its center and radius are
$$O_{1}=\left(\frac{c-b}{2},\,\frac{a^{2}-bc}{2a}\right),\qquad R_{1}^{2}=\frac{(a^{2}+b^{2})(a^{2}+c^{2})}{4a^{2}}.$$

**Circumcircle $\Omega$ of $AXY$.** Write $x^{2}+y^{2}+px+qy+r=0$ with $r=-a^{2}-qa$ (from $A$). Substituting $X$ and $Y$ and simplifying (using $x_{1}^{2}+y_{1}^{2}=4a^{4}/(a^{2}+c^{2})$, $x_{2}^{2}+y_{2}^{2}=4a^{4}/(a^{2}+b^{2})$) gives two linear equations in $p,q$. Eliminating $q$:
$$a^{2}(b-c)+p(a^{2}-bc)=0\implies p=\frac{a^{2}(c-b)}{a^{2}-bc},$$
and back-substitution gives $q=\dfrac{a(bc-3a^{2})}{a^{2}-bc}$, $\ r=\dfrac{2a^{4}}{a^{2}-bc}$. Hence
$$O_{2}=\left(\frac{a^{2}(b-c)}{2(a^{2}-bc)},\,\frac{a(3a^{2}-bc)}{2(a^{2}-bc)}\right),$$
and
$$R_{2}^{2}=\frac{p^{2}+q^{2}}{4}-r=\frac{a^{2}(a^{2}+b^{2})(a^{2}+c^{2})}{4(a^{2}-bc)^{2}}=\frac{a^{4}}{(a^{2}-bc)^{2}}\,R_{1}^{2},$$
where the key simplification uses
$$a^{2}(b-c)^{2}+(bc-3a^{2})^{2}-8a^{2}(a^{2}-bc)=(a^{2}+b^{2})(a^{2}+c^{2}).$$
Thus $R_{2}=\dfrac{a^{2}}{a^{2}-bc}\,R_{1}$ (positive by $(\star)$).

**Distance between centers.**
$$\Delta x=\frac{c-b}{2}-\frac{a^{2}(b-c)}{2(a^{2}-bc)}=\frac{(c-b)(2a^{2}-bc)}{2(a^{2}-bc)},$$
$$\Delta y=\frac{a^{2}-bc}{2a}-\frac{a(3a^{2}-bc)}{2(a^{2}-bc)}=\frac{(a^{2}-bc)^{2}-a^{2}(3a^{2}-bc)}{2a(a^{2}-bc)}=\frac{-(2a^{2}-bc)(a^{2}+bc)}{2a(a^{2}-bc)},$$
using $2a^{4}+a^{2}bc-b^{2}c^{2}=(2a^{2}-bc)(a^{2}+bc)$. Therefore
$$|O_{1}O_{2}|^{2}=\frac{(2a^{2}-bc)^{2}}{4(a^{2}-bc)^{2}}\left[(c-b)^{2}+\frac{(a^{2}+bc)^{2}}{a^{2}}\right].$$
The bracket equals
$$(b^{2}-2bc+c^{2})+\frac{a^{4}+2a^{2}bc+b^{2}c^{2}}{a^{2}}=a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}+\frac{b^{2}c^{2}}{a^{2}}=\frac{(a^{2}+b^{2})(a^{2}+c^{2})}{a^{2}}=4R_{1}^{2}.$$
Hence
$$|O_{1}O_{2}|^{2}=\frac{(2a^{2}-bc)^{2}}{(a^{2}-bc)^{2}}\,R_{1}^{2},\qquad |O_{1}O_{2}|=\frac{2a^{2}-bc}{a^{2}-bc}\,R_{1},$$
where $2a^{2}-bc>0$ and $a^{2}-bc>0$ both hold by $(\star)$.

**Tangency.** Finally,
$$R_{1}+R_{2}=R_{1}\left(1+\frac{a^{2}}{a^{2}-bc}\right)=R_{1}\cdot\frac{2a^{2}-bc}{a^{2}-bc}=|O_{1}O_{2}|.$$
So the distance between the centers of $\Gamma$ (circumcircle of $ABC$) and $\Omega$ (circumcircle of $AXY$) equals the sum of their radii: the two circles are externally tangent. $\blacksquare$

